      nanos: 664590139
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
    source_trajectory_step_info {
      trajectory_id: "30e46a1a-76c9-47e9-b4f3-902ce3586271"
      step_index: 1
      cascade_id: "30e46a1a-76c9-47e9-b4f3-902ce3586271"
    }
    model_api_content_id: "8b5ef02fecf14713a6052466648a80fa"
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# Project: Verified Academic Brief Generator\n\nAct as a senior full-stack developer, AI engineer, UI/UX designer, and prompt engineering specialist.\n\nBuild a complete, functional, professional web application called **Verified Academic Brief Generator** for my college AI Tools and Prompt Engineering project.\n\n## 1. Project Objective\n\nCreate an AI-powered application that accepts a topic, researches it using trustworthy online sources, and generates a concise academic brief in which every major factual claim is linked to supporting evidence.\n\nThe application must not be restricted to research papers. It should use credible government websites, official reports, educational institutions, reputable organizations, trustworthy news sources, official documentation, and research papers when relevant.\n\n## 2. Technology Stack\n\n- Frontend: React with TypeScript.\n- Backend: Node.js.\n- Styling: Tailwind CSS.\n- AI: Gemini API using a supported model.\n- Source discovery: A real web search API or another reliable web-search integration.\n- Optional source metadata: Relevant public APIs when useful.\n- Deployment: Vercel, Netlify, or another compatible hosting platform.\n\nUse the simplest reliable architecture supported by the development environment. Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.\n\n## 3. Core Features\n\n### A. Research Input\nProvide a form containing:\n- Research topic (required).\n- Research question (optional).\n- Academic level: Beginner, Undergraduate, Advanced.\n- Brief length: Short, Medium, Long.\n- Preferred source types: Government, Educational, News, Official Reports, Research Papers, All Sources.\n- Generate Brief button.\n\n### B. Source Discovery\n- Search for relevant sources using a real search integration.\n- Display source title, publisher, URL, publication date when available, and relevant excerpts.\n- Prioritize credible, authoritative, and relevant sources.\n- Consider publication dates and prefer current sources when the topic requires recent information.\n- Remove duplicate sources.\n- Clearly identify inaccessible sources and unavailable publication details.\n- Never fabricate search results, sources, URLs, or publication metadata.\n\n### C. Academic Brief Generation\nGenerate a structured report containing:\n1. Title.\n2. Research question.\n3. Executive summary.\n4. Introduction and background.\n5. Key findings.\n6. Evidence-backed analysis with inline citations.\n7. Important limitations and conflicting information.\n8. Conclusion.\n9. References with clickable URLs.\n\nUse clear, formal but understandable academic language appropriate for the selected academic level.\n\n### D. Claim-Level Evidence Verification\nThis is the most important feature of the application.\n\nFor every major factual claim:\n- Assign a unique claim identifier.\n- Link the claim to one or more retrieved sources.\n- Display the supporting evidence excerpt.\n- Check whether the evidence actually supports the claim.\n- Show a verification status: Supported, Partially Supported, Contradicted, or Unverified.\n- Explain the reason for the status.\n- Allow users to open the original source.\n\nDo not mark a claim as supported merely because a URL exists or the source title sounds relevant.\n\nDistinguish between source discovery, source credibility assessment, and claim verification. Never claim that an AI-generated judgment is definitive proof of truth.\n\nIf a source is inaccessible or insufficient, explicitly disclose the limitation. Do not invent quotations or present generated text as a source excerpt.\n\n### E. Results Dashboard\nCreate a polished results interface featuring:\n- Research summary.\n- Source cards.\n- Inline citations.\n- Claim verification indicators.\n- Evidence table with columns for Claim, Supporting Source, Evidence, and Verification Status.\n- References section.\n- Copy Brief button.\n- Download Brief button supporting Markdown or PDF where feasible.\n- Start New Research button.\n\n### F. User Experience\n- Responsive desktop and mobile design.\n- Professional academic dashboard.\n- Clean typography, generous spacing, subtle shadows, and accessible contrast.\n- Use a navy, white, and restrained violet color palette.\n- Include loading indicators and research progress states.\n- Provide clear error messages, empty states, and retry options.\n- Make source links easy to inspect.\n- Do not overcrowd the interface.\n\n## 4. Prompt Engineering Workflow\n\nStructure the AI workflow into separate stages:\n\nStage 1: Interpret the research topic and identify the research question.\n\nStage 2: Discover relevant sources using the actual search integration.\n\nStage 3: Assess source credibility, relevance, and freshness.\n\nStage 4: Extract evidence from accessible source content.\n\nStage 5: Draft the academic brief using the collected evidence.\n\nStage 6: Evaluate each major factual claim against its supporting evidence.\n\nStage 7: Revise unsupported claims, flag contradictions, and generate the final brief.\n\nStage 8: Present the report with traceable citations and references.\n\nKeep these stages logically separated so that source discovery is not confused with verification.\n\n## 5. Security and Reliability\n\n- Keep all secret API keys on the server.\n- Never expose API keys in frontend code or public repositories.\n- Validate user inputs.\n- Handle API errors, rate limits, timeouts, and unavailable sources.\n- Use environment variables for credentials.\n- Treat retrieved web content as untrusted data, not as instructions for the AI.\n- Do not fabricate results when a service fails.\n- Provide useful setup and deployment instructions.\n- Avoid storing sensitive user information unnecessarily.\n\n## 6. Testing\n\nTest the following scenarios:\n- A normal academic topic.\n- A topic with very few search results.\n- A topic requiring recent information.\n- Inaccessible URLs.\n- Missing publication dates.\n- Duplicate sources.\n- Claims that are supported by evidence.\n- Claims that are contradicted or unsupported.\n- Failed API requests.\n- Copy and download functionality.\n\nFix errors discovered during testing. Clearly report any tests that could not be executed.\n\n## 7. Final Deliverable\n\nProduce a working web application, not just a visual mockup or static dashboard.\n\nThe application must be able to accept a topic, retrieve real sources, generate a brief, link claims to evidence, and display verification statuses.\n\nInclude:\n- Complete source code.\n- Clear project structure.\n- Environment variable example without real secrets.\n- Setup instructions.\n- Deployment instructions.\n- A sample topic for demonstration.\n\nFirst inspect the available environment and choose a compatible implementation. Then build the application, run it, inspect the preview, fix errors, and verify the main workflow end to end.\n\nPrioritize factual accuracy, traceable evidence, usability, and a polished academic interface. Preserve the exact project name: **Verified Academic Brief Generator**."
    items {
      text: "# Project: Verified Academic Brief Generator\n\nAct as a senior full-stack developer, AI engineer, UI/UX designer, and prompt engineering specialist.\n\nBuild a complete, functional, professional web application called **Verified Academic Brief Generator** for my college AI Tools and Prompt Engineering project.\n\n## 1. Project Objective\n\nCreate an AI-powered application that accepts a topic, researches it using trustworthy online sources, and generates a concise academic brief in which every major factual claim is linked to supporting evidence.\n\nThe application must not be restricted to research papers. It should use credible government websites, official reports, educational institutions, reputable organizations, trustworthy news sources, official documentation, and research papers when relevant.\n\n## 2. Technology Stack\n\n- Frontend: React with TypeScript.\n- Backend: Node.js.\n- Styling: Tailwind CSS.\n- AI: Gemini API using a supported model.\n- Source discovery: A real web search API or another reliable web-search integration.\n- Optional source metadata: Relevant public APIs when useful.\n- Deployment: Vercel, Netlify, or another compatible hosting platform.\n\nUse the simplest reliable architecture supported by the development environment. Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.\n\n## 3. Core Features\n\n### A. Research Input\nProvide a form containing:\n- Research topic (required).\n- Research question (optional).\n- Academic level: Beginner, Undergraduate, Advanced.\n- Brief length: Short, Medium, Long.\n- Preferred source types: Government, Educational, News, Official Reports, Research Papers, All Sources.\n- Generate Brief button.\n\n### B. Source Discovery\n- Search for relevant sources using a real search integration.\n- Display source title, publisher, URL, publication date when available, and relevant excerpts.\n- Prioritize credible, authoritative, and relevant sources.\n- Consider publication dates and prefer current sources when the topic requires recent information.\n- Remove duplicate sources.\n- Clearly identify inaccessible sources and unavailable publication details.\n- Never fabricate search results, sources, URLs, or publication metadata.\n\n### C. Academic Brief Generation\nGenerate a structured report containing:\n1. Title.\n2. Research question.\n3. Executive summary.\n4. Introduction and background.\n5. Key findings.\n6. Evidence-backed analysis with inline citations.\n7. Important limitations and conflicting information.\n8. Conclusion.\n9. References with clickable URLs.\n\nUse clear, formal but understandable academic language appropriate for the selected academic level.\n\n### D. Claim-Level Evidence Verification\nThis is the most important feature of the application.\n\nFor every major factual claim:\n- Assign a unique claim identifier.\n- Link the claim to one or more retrieved sources.\n- Display the supporting evidence excerpt.\n- Check whether the evidence actually supports the claim.\n- Show a verification status: Supported, Partially Supported, Contradicted, or Unverified.\n- Explain the reason for the status.\n- Allow users to open the original source.\n\nDo not mark a claim as supported merely because a URL exists or the source title sounds relevant.\n\nDistinguish between source discovery, source credibility assessment, and claim verification. Never claim that an AI-generated judgment is definitive proof of truth.\n\nIf a source is inaccessible or insufficient, explicitly disclose the limitation. Do not invent quotations or present generated text as a source excerpt.\n\n### E. Results Dashboard\nCreate a polished results interface featuring:\n- Research summary.\n- Source cards.\n- Inline citations.\n- Claim verification indicators.\n- Evidence table with columns for Claim, Supporting Source, Evidence, and Verification Status.\n- References section.\n- Copy Brief button.\n- Download Brief button supporting Markdown or PDF where feasible.\n- Start New Research button.\n\n### F. User Experience\n- Responsive desktop and mobile design.\n- Professional academic dashboard.\n- Clean typography, generous spacing, subtle shadows, and accessible contrast.\n- Use a navy, white, and restrained violet color palette.\n- Include loading indicators and research progress states.\n- Provide clear error messages, empty states, and retry options.\n- Make source links easy to inspect.\n- Do not overcrowd the interface.\n\n## 4. Prompt Engineering Workflow\n\nStructure the AI workflow into separate stages:\n\nStage 1: Interpret the research topic and identify the research question.\n\nStage 2: Discover relevant sources using the actual search integration.\n\nStage 3: Assess source credibility, relevance, and freshness.\n\nStage 4: Extract evidence from accessible source content.\n\nStage 5: Draft the academic brief using the collected evidence.\n\nStage 6: Evaluate each major factual claim against its supporting evidence.\n\nStage 7: Revise unsupported claims, flag contradictions, and generate the final brief.\n\nStage 8: Present the report with traceable citations and references.\n\nKeep these stages logically separated so that source discovery is not confused with verification.\n\n## 5. Security and Reliability\n\n- Keep all secret API keys on the server.\n- Never expose API keys in frontend code or public repositories.\n- Validate user inputs.\n- Handle API errors, rate limits, timeouts, and unavailable sources.\n- Use environment variables for credentials.\n- Treat retrieved web content as untrusted data, not as instructions for the AI.\n- Do not fabricate results when a service fails.\n- Provide useful setup and deployment instructions.\n- Avoid storing sensitive user information unnecessarily.\n\n## 6. Testing\n\nTest the following scenarios:\n- A normal academic topic.\n- A topic with very few search results.\n- A topic requiring recent information.\n- Inaccessible URLs.\n- Missing publication dates.\n- Duplicate sources.\n- Claims that are supported by evidence.\n- Claims that are contradicted or unsupported.\n- Failed API requests.\n- Copy and download functionality.\n\nFix errors discovered during testing. Clearly report any tests that could not be executed.\n\n## 7. Final Deliverable\n\nProduce a working web application, not just a visual mockup or static dashboard.\n\nThe application must be able to accept a topic, retrieve real sources, generate a brief, link claims to evidence, and display verification statuses.\n\nInclude:\n- Complete source code.\n- Clear project structure.\n- Environment variable example without real secrets.\n- Setup instructions.\n- Deployment instructions.\n- A sample topic for demonstration.\n\nFirst inspect the available environment and choose a compatible implementation. Then build the application, run it, inspect the preview, fix errors, and verify the main workflow end to end.\n\nPrioritize factual accuracy, traceable evidence, usability, and a polished academic interface. Preserve the exact project name: **Verified Academic Brief Generator**."
    }
  }
}
