            lines {
              text: "  \'post-quantum-cryptography\': {"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    id: \'brief-pqc-02\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    title: \'Post-Quantum Cryptography: NIST Standards, Migration Complexity, and Public Key Vulnerabilities\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    topic: \'Quantum Computing vs Post-Quantum Cryptography Standards NIST\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    researchQuestion: \'What are the technical and operational challenges associated with transitioning critical cyberinfrastructure from classical public key algorithms to NIST-standardized Post-Quantum Cryptographic algorithms?\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    academicLevel: \'advanced\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    briefLength: \'medium\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    generatedAt: \'2026-03-20T14:15:00Z\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    executiveSummary: \'Shor\342\200\231s algorithm poses an existential vulnerability to widely deployed asymmetric cryptographic primitives, including RSA and Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC). In August 2024, the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) released its initial set of finalized post-quantum standards: ML-KEM (FIPS 203), ML-DSA (FIPS 204), and SLH-DSA (FIPS 205). While these lattice-based and stateless hash-based algorithms offer robust theoretical protection against fault-tolerant quantum computers, real-world deployment faces severe friction. Key challenges include orders-of-magnitude larger public keys and ciphertext sizes, legacy protocol buffer overruns in TLS handshakes, algorithmic side-channel vulnerabilities, and the urgent phenomenon of \"Harvest Now, Decrypt Later\" (HNDL) espionage.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    introduction: \'The cryptographic security underpinning modern global finance, internet communications, and defense telemetry relies fundamentally on the computational intractability of integer factorization and the discrete logarithm problem. Peter Shor\342\200\231s 1994 quantum polynomial-time algorithm demonstrated that a sufficiently fault-tolerant quantum computer running error-corrected qubits would compromise standard RSA-2048 and ECC-256 within minutes. Consequently, NIST initiated an 8-year global standardization effort to identify, stress-test, and standardize quantum-resistant replacements based on alternative mathematical problems, predominantly Module Learning with Errors (M-LWE).\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    keyFindings: ["
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'In August 2024, NIST formally released FIPS 203 (ML-KEM), FIPS 204 (ML-DSA), and FIPS 205 (SLH-DSA), designating them as the primary post-quantum cryptographic standards [S1].\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'ML-KEM-768 ciphertext sizes (1,088 bytes) and public keys (1,184 bytes) represent an approximate 35x expansion compared to X25519 elliptic-curve public keys [S2].\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Nation-state threat actors are actively conducting \"Harvest Now, Decrypt Later\" (HNDL) data exfiltration of high-value encrypted traffic with decades-long strategic shelf-lives [S3].\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Hybrid key exchange mechanisms combining X25519 with ML-KEM (X25519MLKEM768) have been implemented in Chromium and OpenSSL to guard against both classical and quantum regression [S4].\'"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    ],"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    evidenceAnalysis: \'The operational integration of post-quantum standards exposes deep architectural assumptions in legacy protocol stacks. As detailed in NIST FIPS 203, ML-KEM relies on the hardness of the Module Learning with Errors problem over cyclotomic rings [S1]. While mathematically resilient, the parameter sizes require substantial bandwidth adaptations. IETF Internet-Draft measurements documented that packet fragmentation occurred in 4.7% of enterprise TLS 1.3 handshakes when raw post-quantum certificates exceeded standard Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU) thresholds of 1,500 bytes [S2].\\n\\nTo address transition risks, federal directives from the US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) and the White House National Security Memorandum-10 mandated cryptographic inventory baselining and prioritized hybrid key encapsulation [S3]. Hybrid deployments ensure that even if unexpected mathematical cryptanalysis emerges against newly standardized lattice schemes, classical elliptic-curve security guarantees remain intact as a fallback [S4]. However, side-channel power analysis during private key expansion in low-power IoT microcontrollers remains an active research concern, necessitating masked hardware implementations [S5].\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    limitationsAndConflictingInfo: \'Conflicting projections exist regarding the cryptographic relevance horizon\342\200\224the estimated timeline until a fault-tolerant quantum computer capable of breaking RSA-2048 (requiring approximately 20 million physical qubits under surface-code architectures) is realized. While conservative estimates place this milestone in the 2035\342\200\2232040 timeframe, aggressive commercial roadmaps cite 2030. Furthermore, claims that transitioning to post-quantum cryptography induces unacceptable latency overhead are contradicted by real-world CDN telemetry from Cloudflare and Google, which demonstrates sub-15ms median connection handshake deltas for hybrid ML-KEM.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    conclusion: \'The transition to post-quantum cryptography represents the largest cryptographic migration in digital history. Organizations must adopt cryptographic agility, migrate to NIST FIPS 203/204 hybrid suites, and audit legacy hardware for MTU packet handling capacity.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    sources: ["
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "      {"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "        id: \'S1\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "        title: \'FIPS 203: Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism Standard\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
